WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.569
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.569 --> 00:00:09.059
Style transfer is an interesting problem in
machine learning research where we have two

00:00:09.059 --> 00:00:15.540
input images, one for content, and one for
style, and the output is our content image

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.590
reimagined with this new style.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:23.001
The cool part is that the content can be a
photo straight from our camera, and the style

00:00:23.001 --> 00:00:28.260
can be a painting, which leads to super fun,
and really good looking results.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:33.179
We have seen plenty of papers doing variations
of style transfer, but can we can push this

00:00:33.179 --> 00:00:34.920
concept further?

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.399
And the answer is, yes!

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:41.960
For instance, few people know that style transfer
can also be done in 3D!

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:47.030
If you look here, you see an artist performing
this style transfer by drawing on a simple

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:53.569
sphere and get their artistic style to carry
over to a complicated piece of 3D geometry.

00:00:53.569 --> 00:00:59.079
We talked about this technique in Two Minute
Papers episode 94, and for your reference,

00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:02.019
we are currently at over episode 340.

00:01:02.019 --> 00:01:04.800
Leave a comment if you’ve been around back
then!

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.730
And this previous technique led to truly amazing
results, but still had two weak points.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.950
One, it took too long.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:17.570
As you see here, this method took around a
minute or more to produce these results.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:23.210
And hold on to your papers, because this new
paper is approximately a 1000 times faster

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:29.870
than that, which means that it can produce
100 frames per second using a whopping 4K

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:30.870
resolution.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:36.280
But of course, none of this matters… if
the visual quality is not similar.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:41.300
And, if you look closely, you see that the
new results are indeed really close to the

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.900
reference results of the older method.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.400
So, what was the other problem?

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.810
The other problem was the lack of temporal
coherence.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:54.660
This means that when creating an animation,
it seems like each of the individual frames

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.070
of the animation were drawn separately by
an artist.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:04.360
In this new work, this is not only eliminated
as you see here, but the new technique even

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.830
gives us the opportunity to control the amount
of flickering.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:14.069
With these improvements, this is is now a
proper tool to help artists perform this 3D

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:20.239
style transfer and create these rich virtual
worlds much quicker and easier in the future.

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:26.109
It also opens up the possibility for novices
to do that, which is an amazing value proposition.

00:02:26.109 --> 00:02:31.189
Limitations still apply, for instance, if
we have a texture with some regularity, such

00:02:31.189 --> 00:02:36.680
as this brickwall pattern here, the alignment
and continuity of the bricks on the 3D model

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.020
may suffer.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.359
This can be fixed, but it is a little labor-intensive.

00:02:40.359 --> 00:02:46.390
However, you know our saying, two more papers
down the line, and this will likely cease

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:47.609
to be an issue.

00:02:47.609 --> 00:02:51.970
And what you’ve seen here today is just
one paper down the line from the original

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:56.840
work, and we can do 4K resolution at a 100
frames per second.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:57.890
Unreal.

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:01.019
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

